## Exact call chain and contract limit

Shared `scripts/campaign/shell.gd:543–595` creates and adds an `own_world_3d` viewport, stage, level and Hero, then immediately calls `enter_restore_candidate`. The two deferred operation-drain callbacks at 185–216 happen **before** this new world exists and therefore establish no registration boundary for it. Fresh disk load at 101–103 likewise runs synchronously inside `_ready`.

`CinderLevel.enter_restore_candidate` (112–136) synchronously returns bool; its owned hook returns String. Construction must remain paused/nonplayable and must precede pure whole-unit validation. Act3 builds its permanent floor in `crystalman_level.gd:55–79`, before restore entry. But its restore hook (419+) calls child `prepare_parent_restore`; `crystalman_approach_unit.gd:689+` calls `prepare_sources`; `crystalman_approach_codec.gd:128+` immediately calls full `record_error`, which reaches Scheduler `snapshot_error` at119. Thus delaying only the later Shell `_snapshot_problem` check would miss the already failing constructor check.

Scheduler `_decode_adapter` at2317 short-circuits `_floor_error` **or** `_floor_path_supported`. `_floor_error` (1502+) checks actual ready collider, shared world, layer, shape, geometry and feet height. `_floor_path_supported` (1536+) proves the complete inset rectangle interval, then explicitly confirms current physics registration using two mask1 native rays and exact floor-body RID membership at1574+. Its comment deliberately distinguishes registration probes from continuous support proof. Do not drop either component, accept empty hits, enlarge tolerances, or substitute saved signatures for actual native queries.

There is no safe place to insert an `await` into the current synchronous pure validator. Attempts calls `snapshot_validator.call(...) -> String` at359; SaveStore invokes payload validation synchronously before commit/read fallback. Shell `_snapshot_problem` at680 discards old candidates and may build another; an ID-only prewarm cache would be incorrect. Player→physical sources→Scheduler→consumers quiet commit also remains nonyielding.

## Recommended smallest next step and conditional API direction

Use Act3's already-owned bounded native diagnostic; do not duplicate its engine job. At the exact failed candidate, separately record `_floor_error` on the adjusted saved feet, inset coverage, Scheduler/floor/Hero World3D identity, floor body RID and physics space, and **both exact existing ray results** (empty, foreign hit or expected floor RID). Repeat only after the proposed paused registration boundary, retaining the same nodes/shape resources/transforms/packet and paused gameplay clocks. A same-frame deferred callback alone is not evidence that physics registration occurred. Never unpause to make the test pass. Do not call `enter_restore_candidate` twice on a partially entered candidate; use direct read-only diagnostics there, then a distinct exact fresh candidate with the tested boundary before its single restore entry for constructor/commit proof.

If native registration is confirmed, Integration owns the shared fix: add a bounded **paused preparation phase outside Attempts/SaveStore validators**, after the raw candidate's immutable floor/world nodes exist and before its synchronous restore entry. For this Act3 case the floor already exists in `_ready`, so the owner need not weaken Scheduler or alter layout. Retain exact candidate/native identity and exact whole-snapshot custody, suppress input/progression/simulation, then perform unchanged synchronous construction, fresh pure validation and nonyielding quiet commit. Every later validator invocation must use the correct exact prepared candidate; no stale ID cache or “previously accepted” stamp. Registration failure disposes only the candidate, preserving donor, attempts and disk. Floors created by another level's restore hook need their own explicit construction/registration ordering; this case does not prove them ready. A barrier's exact kind and bounded duration must follow actual diagnostic evidence, not a guessed one-frame guarantee.

This touches more than `_prepare`: startup `load_saved`, synchronous payload validation, retry revalidation and disposal/reentry all constrain that phase. Until the diagnostic closes, prefer a scoped diagnostic over a speculative async conversion or global PhysicsServer manipulation.

